    description: "Zenodo archive of version 1.0.4"
date-released: 2026-05-09
repository-code: "https://github.com/jespergran98/prime-line-cover"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  An exact branch-and-bound solver for the minimum line cover of
  prime-indexed points (OEIS A373813). The minimum number of straight
  lines covering N prime points (k, p_k) defines f(N); every line through
  three or more such points encodes an exact arithmetic coincidence among
  prime values, making f(N) computationally hard to certify exactly as N
  grows. The solver certifies f(1814) = 229 as the current computational
  record, extending the prior certified boundary from N = 861 — reached in
  approximately 282 hours via a general-purpose MIP solver — first to N =
  1024 in under 40 hours, and then to N = 1814 with 790 further certified,
  strictly optimal terms in 461 hours 35 minutes on a Google Cloud
  c4d-highcpu-8 instance (8 vCPUs, 15 GB RAM); the N = 861 milestone is
  reached in approximately 22 minutes. At startup, all heavy lines (those
  through at least three prime points) are enumerated in 50 ms and stored
  as 2048-bit bitmasks, of which 30,646 are active at N = 1814; the
  incremental sweep carries forward a primal warm-start solution, a
  Lagrangian dual seed, and a witness cover from f(N-1) at each step. If
  the witness covers the new point (N, p_N), optimality is certified
  instantly (mode W); otherwise a cover-inequality floor is established at
  the root and exact branch-and-bound is invoked — root bound closure
  (mode R) or full parallel search (mode D). Per-node pruning uses a
  Lagrangian gain bound tightened by projected subgradient descent with
  coordinate-descent polishing. The Exclusive Dependency Rule forces into
  the partial solution any productive heavy line for which at least three
  uncovered points each lie on no other productive heavy line — without
  loss of optimality. When the lower bound is within one of the incumbent
  and at least 64 points remain uncovered, strong branching evaluates at
  most four candidate lines via Lagrangian simulation; independent
  subproblems are dispatched to worker threads via a shared atomic task
  queue. The 790-term extension identifies 86 new awkward primes (N = 1063
  through 1811, cover sizes 144 to 229; OEIS A393445), bringing the total
  at or below N = 1814 to 229. Every reported value is certified optimal;
  the solver has no heuristic stopping rule. The sweep stopped at N = 1814
  because the Google Cloud free-trial credit funding the instance was
  exhausted, not because of an architectural limit; the 2048-bit mask
  leaves 234 further indices reachable with more compute and no source
  change.
keywords:
